Before installing a SIEM it's worth checking the machines can actually reach each other. 
A Wazuh agent that cant talk to the manager just produces nothing, and if I hadnt tested 
the network first I'd have ended up debugging Wazuh when the real problem was a layer underneath it.

Addresses

The SOC server is on 192.168.65.4 and the attacker is on 192.168.65.5. 
Both use interface enp0s1. The victim doesn't exist yet.
Both are on 192.168.65.0/24, which is a virtual network UTM creates on the host. 
Addresses come from DHCP.

The interface being called enp0s1 instead of eth0 is systemd's predictable naming. 
The name gets derived from where the device sits on the bus, so it doesn't 
shuffle around between reboots the way eth0 and eth1 used to.

FINDING THEM:   [  ip a  ]

On the SOC server: 2:     enp0s1: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500
                          inet 192.168.65.4/24 brd 192.168.65.255 scope global dynamic enp0s1


Ubuntu Server also prints it in the login banner 
so most of the time I don't need this command: [ IPv4 address for enp0s1: 192.168.65.4 ]

Testing it:
ping sends ICMP echo requests and waits for replies. 
If replies come back the two machines can reach each other.

I tested it both ways. One direction working doesn't tell you the other one does. 
A firewall rule or NAT config can easily allow traffic one way and drop
it the other, and Wazuh needs the agent to reach the manager specifically, 
thereofre direction matters here.

