What the output shows: 

0% packet loss is the number that matters, and both directions came back clean.

The ttl=64 is worth a mention. TTL starts at 64 on Linux and drops by one every router it passes through.
It arrived at 64, so it went through zero routers, which means these two are on the same 
network segment rather than being routed to each other. That's what I wanted.

Times in the low single digit milliseconds are normal for two VMs on the same physical machine.

Things that could have gone wrong: 

If the two VMs had come back on different subnets, say one on 192.168.65.x and the other on 10.0.2.x, 
they'd be on separate virtual networks and wouldn't reach each other without routing between them. 
The fix is putting both on the same network mode in UTM.

100% packet loss would mean either the target's off, they're on different networks,
or something's dropping ICMP. Worth checking ufw status in that case, because a host firewall
can block ping while still letting everything else through, so a failed ping doesn't always mean the network is broken.

The addresses come from DHCP so they can change. If something that worked yesterday stops working, check ip a before assuming 
anything's actually wrong.

